## A. Seven tracked passages

1. **ch38:13 → gone (38:11).** "It was the first thing that she had said, and it was a healer's question." is cut. Her question stands by itself and he answers it.
2. **ch39:31.** "Then it's hers," said Lira. The narrator's close is cut. Karis makes one mark in her notebook; Brom, who has been waiting his turn, raises his eyes and asks what the new one does.
3. **ch40 scene 4 (was 198–258) — re-entered (now 223–257).**
   - The writing of the birthday line, with "A stop… Good. Write it down before it changes.", now closes the table scene (40:197–219), which ends on somebody knocking the frost off his boots outside.
   - The new scene opens on Gault's line, already in his head (40:223), fetched by the word *instruments* as **Brom** reads Pike's bark aloud, once, in his slow voice; Cael's hands were full of a book and a pencil. The protected copy is printed once, as Brom's reading (40:231). Cael does not read it over or pick words out of it. The memory of the leaf (in the west, never in the satchel) and one paragraph on the two men follow; then **Seln checks the count** with the bark held to the light ("Six went in. Six came out… He's counted her." 40:239; "So has the broker," 40:243). The carter gives the clerk's copy and the westbound boat, asks whether it is wrong, and is answered. Oryn's "It's changed, then." stands.
   - **Hesk's parcel is out of the doorway.** It is found at the cleared board, at the end of the apples scene (40:309–313): he lifts Hesk's satchel to put the bark away, and the satchel was the last parcel, with the last note under its flap, and nothing beside it. No look at the door, no companions answering with spoons. "Nobody at that board had spoken the old man's name all evening, and he did not speak it now."
4. **ch40:319 → 40:325.** The first self-reading opens with the thing coming home to his own two fingers and his start of surprise; the hands, the breath and the count are given in one retrospective paragraph; then the first search for a bank. The solitude (the waiting, the dark lantern, the boots off, four people asleep within six feet) comes after the finding (40:363). "He did it that night, alone, as she had set it." is gone.
5. **ch41:245 → 41:207–233.** The scene was written out again. It opens on the result: the leaf goes back into the box clean, she stoppers the ink, wipes the nib, and boots are on the ladder. What woke her, the three leaves in a row, the known sentence and both her reasons follow in the past; the pen held over the page is told once, near the end, as ink drying on the nib and being dipped again. It closes on "There was not a word on the leaf, and it was in the box." The hypothesis is not stated.
6. **ch43:85.** The three paragraphs that set Cael's withheld sentence beside hers are cut from the barn. Karis finishes; the others go up; she gives him the three facts. His parallel is said only on the gate-walk (43, last scene: "Karis has a thing that she won't write. I've a thing that I won't write."), and in the protected second pole. The scene's last exchange is now "I'll not ask you what the fear is." / "No. You have not, in four days, and I had noticed…" (43:109–111), with the leaf that he saw go into the box named once as his reason.

## B. ch41 runs forward

The night of the 103rd now stands before noon of the 104th. Order: the ring laid flat (the 103rd, by day) · Karis's count (that night, 41:65) · Oryn at the barn door and the bowman (noon of the 104th, 41:103) · the word (that evening, 41:163, headed "the evening of that same day, the hundred and fourth") · the leaf and the pen (before light on the 105th, 41:207) · the word said aloud (that morning, 41:237). Nothing was cut for the reordering. Karis's window is now in two parts (one scene; then two). "One basket" and the apple's effect on her method are kept: she says it to the crew by day, and that night it becomes the cup and the bowl.

## C. The thesis said once

It is said in full at its first occasion, the count on the night of the 103rd (what she will swear to in the cup; what only a second floor can settle in the bowl). After that the explanation is cut wherever an image had already landed. Every finding stands.

- **41, the count:** "It was the truest thing on the floor." and the long paragraph on the small bald man's sentence are cut to two sentences ("She could still taste that apple…"); the coordinator's wordings are untouched.
- **41, the word:** the simile of the column that comes out even, "she had been taught to", and "she went over it twice more to be sure" are cut; what the name claims is kept as three short sentences.
