editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:13:"One. A bar of iron of the registry's pattern, bearing its stamp and the number thirty-one, stands on the edge of the Quieting at the Fallow Ring, a boot's width from the place where I found that edge with my feet. Two. By its rust, and by the turf grown against it, it has stood there about five years. Three. It was driven at the far end of a measure, so that whoever drove it had found the same circle that I paced, and had a cord or a chain to find it with. Four. I do not know what the number counts, or what was written down afterward, or where it is kept."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:115:The healer read the arm at first light on the hundred and tenth day, in the yard, with the frost still white on the well.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:121:He had done the sum on the ladder, and he did it again now, since he could not help it. If the eighth mark had turned, there would be a mending to pay for it, and then more days of watching on top of that. Every one of them would come off a round that was already sixteen behind, and be laid against a girl who had been told that she was not to carry them and would carry them regardless. If it had not turned, a healer went north in the morning.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:151:"You I can tell nothing. You'd know before I would." She rubbed her palms together. "That's sixteen out of this gate. A round never gives a day back; it takes one."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:153:He had that sum by heart, from the ninety-fifth day to the morning after a fortnight. Sixteen and one made seventeen, somewhere on a road that he had never seen; and she did not say where the one would fall.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:159:Cael had not seen that it was a matter. He had known for eleven days that three boxes stood in that loft at a hundredweight each, that the carter who had brought them east was gone home with grain, and that the ferry was two days off. He had put all of it where he put the things that would be dealt with when there was leisure, behind a table and a birthday and a week of slips.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:191:"She goes on the low side of nothing. You go on the low side of her. She'll not cross moving water with anybody on her near hand, and it's no use arguing; she was like that when I got her. Her near hind's been shod since the Oxhollow road, and it'll want looking at by the thaw. She answers to a click." She showed him, with her tongue. "She's never had a name, and she's not to be given one. It's a beast. You name a thing and then you're sorry for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:277:He gave it to her in order, and low, and he began by telling her that it had not started on a white floor. It had begun at noon on that day, a cord north of the grey stone, while five people were looking at what they had lost and got back; he had opened the read a pace short of the line, and walked, and it had run across the stride without a break. He had gone twenty strides in, to a boulder, and asked for a foot of Wind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:313:"The second one. You're allowed to say so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:325:"There's one thing more," he said. "I read myself, on the night of the birthday. I found something. I can't give it to you. It's the healer's to hear before anybody; that was her price, and I'm paying it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-43.md:329:She said it at once, and without any hurt in it; and he heard her hand move. It came along the plank between them in the dark, the right one, which was allowed to hold things; and it stopped against the side of his boot, and stayed there, as it had once stayed for a breath on the top of his head in a loft.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:13:"Lira's had that since dusk. The rest of you have it now. It's fourteen days late, and I'm sorry for every one of them." He looked at the square woman by the ladder. "And there's one thing more that I have, from the night of the birthday, which none of you is getting. I read myself, as she set it. What I found is hers before it's anybody's. I'm telling you that it exists, so that nobody learns later that I sat on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:31:"His face," said Lira. "On the floor, a foot from mine, over the dog's head. He was choosing. I didn't know between what. Then in the lane the grass leaned on my left hand with nobody in it. And then the air cracked, twenty feet north, and it was my crack."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:61:"One: it is a thing that happens, and I have called it the Quieting. Two: five sigils of five Paths failed at one stride, in one way, and came back so. Three: the floor is older than the oldest stone that I can date. Four: the registry has put iron on the edge of it." She shut the notebook on her finger. "And there is a hypothesis, which is mine, and which is not entered anywhere."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:95:"That's how you find a handful. One at a time, for money." She nodded toward Hesk's satchel on its nail, where a broker's cream paper lay folded in a book. "Somebody paid a hundred marks to learn whether a crew would come out of the first, and I do not think that he has stopped wanting to know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:107:"North in the morning," Oryn said. It was not an answer to Karis; it was a fact about a route. "Oxhollow's seventeen days late and the old woman's knee doesn't care what's under a floor. I'll be on the road at first light and I'll be three days north by the time you're back at the board." She looked at him. "Thornwater's on my route. You know where that is now."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:189:"And you've not gone back to it in eight days. I'm not asking you why, since I can see why from here. So you'll go back. You'll read it three times, on three different days, with a night between each. Read it frightened, and read it when you're not, and then you'll know what's there and what was only you." She buckled the strap. "After that you tell me, all of it at once and in order. You don't give me a piece of it now because I'm leaving and you'd like to have paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:215:She slung the pannier on her back and went to the mule, and laid her hand once on the brown neck. Then she walked away across the yard toward the northern fence, on the low side of nothing, and she went through the wicket without a glance behind her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:219:*Nothing out here reads me. And at a line you can mark with a rock, every Path in this camp stops, and mine doesn't. Karis won't write her hypothesis. I won't write mine. We're going to find the other circles. Pike said nobody goes in twice. Tomorrow makes three.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:225:Oryn went out by the northern wicket as the hundred and eleventh day was breaking, and for the first quarter of a mile she kept putting her hand out for a cheek-strap.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:235:"Sixteen days too long."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:247:Out of that gate on the hundred and eleventh, when she had said the ninety-fifth: sixteen. Two days of road to the next. Two gates after it, and an old woman on the way whose knee had never once kept to a healer's days; that was where the odd one would go. Then the ford with the oxen, where they had last seen her on the eighty-fourth and had looked for her on the hundred and fourth. She would come down to it on the hundred and twenty-first, if nothing broke, and that was seventeen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:253:Seventeen days, then, in which she did not know. She had told a crew at a fire what late was. She had not told them that the knowing of it never grew lighter, however often a round came out safe.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:295:She wanted that. She looked squarely at the wanting, with her back against a cold stone, as she had once looked at it on a road out of Oxhollow. She had not forgiven him. She did not expect that she would, quite. But she had once been hungry for sixteen days to know how it was that he could not be read, and she was hungrier now to hear what he had found.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:299:It had gone north from the wicket, and over the low place, and north again. At the first water it turned along the bank, as a carter's road will, and by the middle of the day she had the sun on her left cheek, and by the afternoon it was in her eyes. The water grew. Another came into it, and a third. There were alders, and then a hedge, and a place where wheels had been in and out of the stream for a great many years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-44.md:303:It was a slow heavy knocking, a long way down the valley, with water falling under it. She knew that sound. There would be a wheel at the end of it, and a fence, and a keeper who counted people off a ridge. Beyond the fence there would be a bench, with whoever had sat down on it sixteen mornings ago when her mule should have come in sight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:75:She blotted it, and he watched the ink go dull. He owed her more than that row, and he had known so since a far lip; and he owed it to one person before her, who was pinning a strip of paper to a clay floor with her right hand and had said *later* ten days ago.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:113:"I'll read it at the fortnight," she said, over her shoulder. "That's the hundred and tenth. If it's taken by then, it's taken."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:117:"I did, and it's sixteen: from the ninety-fifth to the morning after your fortnight." The pannier came down. "I can't read your arm from the next gate."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:209:"Oldest to the right," she said. "This first is a district hall's, and I had it off the step myself; it was dressed in my grandmother's time. The pale one is from a library that I used to be allowed into, and the honey-coloured is Ostrand's wall. And that is the oldest dressed stone between Denvash and the Line that anybody can put a year to. A mason was re-laying a drain at the foot of the old gate, the one that the city counts its years from. I call it the founding stone, and I have never owned anything older."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:237:"A knife marks a stone. On the afternoon of the ninety-sixth, while Lira was pinning the fourth sheet, I put this point to the outside of that rim, low down on the north, where a scratch would spoil nothing, and I leaned on it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:253:She had Lira lift two strips to the light, side by side: the stone that had faced south-east, and the stone that had faced due north.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:255:"It is the same band, cut to the same depth; a fingernail, he said, and I measured it." On the southern strip the lines stood sharp and white. On the northern they were grey and narrow, and in two places the pencil had not found them at all. "That is weather. That is a north wind on the Fallow, taking a little off a stone in every cold term. Now the founding stone." She put its rubbing between the two strips. "It has stood in a north wall for as long as that city has had a gate. Look at how much of the tool is left on it, and then look at how much of that band is left on the north."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:269:"It's older, I'll say once and not again, than [UNBOUND] — older than the word they retired, and I can't say by how much. Entered as a limit, not a finding. We explained the middle of the story in Ostrand. This is before the beginning, and I don't have a ruler for it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-42.md:295:He showed her, without a word, by sitting down. He was not a young man, and he did it in stages. He sat on the planks with his left leg folded under him and his right knee drawn up, and the sole of that boot planted; and he laid an invisible stock across the knee, and bent his cheek to it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:35:"This morning there's a tenth." He kept his eyes on her face, which hung over him upside down and grey. "It wants a real thing done to me for a long time, at a cost, by somebody who means it, and you had told me at Oxhollow what the long reading was: both hands, and all of you, for minutes. I knew when I lay down what it was likely to be. At the end there was a place in me standing open, and the thing that you read with was at the door. I could have kept that door shut. I'm better at shutting them than at anything else I do. I opened it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:143:"There's a line in there about what it's to cost you. Whatever I like, you've written, down to my walking north without you on the road. I've been paid in a good many ways out here. I've had a coat, and a cold fowl, and a shoe on the mule. I never yet had a patient hand me his own fine, ready counted, before I'd said what was owing."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:153:It went into him more deeply than anything that she had yet said. He had priced a great many outcomes in a loft: her anger, and her contempt, and a road going north with nobody on it that he knew. He had not once thought that she might simply decline to tell him, and leave the question standing open in him for good, in the one place where no leaf of his could shut it and no price of his could buy it shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:177:"I'm not asking you for a promise. I've seen what you do with what you want. I'm telling you what's owed, and I'll say when."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:179:"Then it's owed, and I've heard you say so."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:193:She did not keep one. She had told the small pale one so across a fire: a healer who wanted a page to know who was owed had stopped looking at them. It was the truest thing that she knew about her trade, and here she sat on her own stool, in her own room, with somebody else's page open on her knee and her thumb in the margin of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:219:So perhaps it had taken nothing that could be weighed. He had told her that it never showed. She believed him; he had been at some pains, that morning, to tell her things that did him no good.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:223:She gave herself away every day. That was all that her trade was. She found where a stranger's current was standing behind a break, and opened the way with her own, and held it open while six weeks of knitting went through in an hour; and she paid for the six weeks. An ox-driver had a foot today because she had been a little older by evening. She had never grudged one of them. It did not come from nowhere, and she had always known where it came from, and had chosen each time to spend it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:245:She could have wept, and she did nothing of the kind. She held a field-hand's leg for the better part of an hour, and paid for it at the rate that such a thing costs, and when she took her hands away there was a pink seam on his shin where a wet red mouth had been since the barley was cut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:263:"He told me. All of it, I think; I'd say he doesn't know how to tell less." Oryn looked at the sling, and at the knot on it, which was her own. "It's a true page. You can say I said so. And you can say that true isn't the same as paid."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:269:She went back to her room. She had a bench to pay that was owed five days now, and a round behind it that was owed a good many more; and a boy in that hold had a piece of her craft in him that he did not know how to use. She was hungry to hear what it would find, and she did not take that for forgiveness. She had been hungry to read him for sixteen days, and it had not made her fond of his ribs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:281:The trough stood by the barn wall, with a skin on it as thick as a slate. He put the heel of his right hand through it, and it was the left that he looked at afterward, turning it over in the cold: the feeling had come back as far as the knuckles in the night, and a hound's weight had dwindled to a yellow mark along the bone. That bill was nearly paid. He had come into this yard with a good many, and one by one a healer had told him that they were done.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-38.md:301:He took his hands out of the water and dried them on his shirt. A healer was sitting on a stool across the yard, behind a shut door, with his leaf on her knee and an answer in her that he would never be given. He had nothing that he could do about either. He had four people in a loft who had told him the truth the night before and had not slept on it, and he owed them the morning, in order, with nothing left out.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:21:The woman's eyebrows went up. She looked over Lira's head at the wicket in the northern fence, beyond which her goats were, and Cael could see her going along them one by one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:67:"Earned, a hundred and thirty-two. In hand, a hundred and eight, less six." The hinge creaked. "A hundred and two. It is the first that I have paid out. And been glad to."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:103:"Two. The green and the brown are one apple." She touched her throat, and paid for the rest of it a few words at a time. "One was picked a month before the other, and you can see it by the stalk."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:189:"Nineteen."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:193:"Nineteen," said Brom, and lifted his cup. Karis creaked it after him. Seln inclined his head.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:209:*Hundred and second day past the Line. Nineteen. Written at Thornwater, in the steading's common room, the fire lit for us. Witnesses: four of my own, and a healer who's a stop.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:231:*Recovery, Fallow road, broker's posting. Taken. Witnessed. Completed: incomplete — instruments not recovered — crew returned. Six went in. Six came out. No price paid.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:237:Gault had signed a figure for him every week for two years, to the decimal, and had ended by writing that no instrument would have such a thing to measure again. Pike had one arm and a horn ink-pot, and had never owned an instrument in his life. Somebody had told him that some were lying at a place, and that a crew had gone to look; and he had put down that they were not there, and that the crew was. Cael thought that he would have liked to see the two men at one table.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:335:He could count, though, by sound and by where each one lay. He went along them, and there were nine that he knew; and the thing that went out from his palm and came back was a tenth, lighter than any of them. He had not been able to hear that one in three days of listening. He could tell it now only as a walker tells his own stride, by the going of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:347:He followed the hiss of Wind down to it, and Wind went round. The millstone went round on the other hand. So did the wick, and the bellows, and the held note, and the thin high one; the low sea parted and went by on both sides and closed beyond. He took each of the ten to that place in turn, one after another, with his heart going hard under his own palm. Not one of them went through.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:351:It was none of the nine, since he had every one of the nine in his ear as it went by. It was not the tenth, since the tenth was the thing that he was going along with, and the tenth went round it as well. He listened into it, and there was no sound in it of any kind. Nothing in it moved, and nothing in it answered him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:371:He ruled a line under that, and wrote the inventory beneath, since a birthday was as good a day as any to count what he carried. There were ten confirmed. He set down the nine in their order, each with the Path that it stood beside and the person it had come from, and against the second of them he wrote *the Bronze from Ardenmere*, as he always did. He set down the tenth: Tide-adjacent, from Oryn; partial; Iron; himself, and whatever his hand was on. It was the first that did nothing in a fight, and the first that he had reached for knowing what he did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:377:*Still open. Still real. Now I can see it. That's worse. The tenth fragment lets me feel my own currents, and there's one in the place session nine logged, and it isn't the tenth, and it isn't any of the nine. It was there before Oryn. It was there before Brom's read caught it. I don't know what it is. I know where it is now. Patience is over; observation begins.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:387:*Daeva's rematch, Reydan's answer — both ride east. Nineteen. Ten fragments. Hesk's package is on a ferry somewhere. The last one said mind the shape. I'm starting to.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:389:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-40.md:391:Below him the yard was white and empty. On the platform by the western gate a lean man stood where he always stood. Cael shut the book on nineteen, and put it under his hand, and lay down beside a wall of other people's barley.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:5:She had waited seven days to do it. Cael had watched her carry that restraint across the Fallow and up a ladder and through a birthday, with eleven sheets lying dry in the dark not six feet from her blanket; and he had understood that it was a kind of manners. She would not look at a thing until she could look at all of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:7:So she had the barn floor swept first. It was a threshing floor of beaten clay, twenty feet by thirty, and Brom went over it twice with a besom while the hold's hens stood by and resented him. She had the sack-menders' trestle carried to the north wall, under the shutter, for the chips and the brass and her three boxes. Then she had a keg set in the middle of the swept clay, and sat down on it, and held out her hand.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:15:"West by north. Lay it there. The foot of it outward."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:55:"I was wrong about apples." Karis rested her throat, and went on. "I looked into one basket, on one day, and I said *two*. A man who had kept those trees for twenty-six years showed me three. This is one basket." Her hand went round the eleven strips. "I have one floor. If a hook is cut a little wide on the ninth stone, I cannot tell whether that is a different kind, or the same kind a little wider. Nobody could, from here. So I shall count. And I shall not number the kinds from one site."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:95:She did want to. She had been waiting all her life for a wall that nobody had read. She had crossed a Line for it, and a mountain, with three boxes; she had knelt on that wall with a pencil while the sun went down, and paid for the last two sheets with a quarter of an hour that a friend had no right to give her. It had never once occurred to her that when the wall came she would not be able to count it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:133:She had the tenth strip across her knee. She had put it as she would have asked the hour.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:137:"I gave a day for this gate, the last time that I gave any, and by that I'd have been out of it on the ninety-fifth. That's nine late here." A thick finger folded down. "There are two days more on that bench before I've paid what I owe it, the way the carts are coming. So I go on the hundred and sixth, and that's eleven late at the next."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:159:It was not kindly said, and it was kindly meant. Lira laid the tenth strip down, and set her four pebbles on it one by one, and got up off the clay on her flat foot.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:193:*the Quieting*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:197:It was made out of a verb, like the other; it named a thing that went on happening, to whatever came over the line, for as long as it stayed there. It did not say how, and it did not say why. It took *the* in front, which made it one thing, and it would take a place after it without complaint: the Quieting at the Fallow Ring. She could write *inside* before it, and *the edge of*, and *a site of*.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-41.md:257:"The Quieting." She made a face. "It sounds like what you do to a baby."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:21:"I'd priced it for her on the leaf. Whatever it cost me, down to her going north without me. She won't have that. She says a patient doesn't hand her his own fine ready counted, and that I'm not the one who knows the price, and shan't be until she does." He drew a breath. "And she knows what she'd have said last night, if I had knocked. She's keeping it. I'm not to be told, today or ever."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:29:"She's set two things on it. I'm to use it on myself before I use it on anybody. And whatever I find, she is the one I tell, on a day that she names." He looked round the four of them. "So if there's anything to find, it's hers before it's yours. I'm saying so now, in order that nobody should think later that I kept a thing back without leave. It's part of what's owed."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:67:He lay between two sacks with Hesk's book under his hand. Lira had brought it up the ladder at dusk inside her coat, and with it one sentence, said exactly as a healer had said it: that it was a true page, and that true was not the same as paid. He had written nothing in it since.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:93:"Nine. Ten. I can't hear the tenth. It's the thing I'm hearing with."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:125:It was also how the five paid for their bread there. Nobody had struck a bargain. The steading's woman had looked at Brom's shoulders on the first evening, and at the height of her loft, and had said that her own men were two short; and Brom had said that he would be glad of something to lift.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:129:Cael carried one at a time. It was the first load that he had had on his back since a mountain, and the ribs took it without a word. He went up past his own blanket at each turn, and past Hesk's satchel on its nail, and set the sack down on a heap that had begun level with his knee and now stood as high as his chest; and by the tenth it had come to him that he was being walled into his own bedroom with other people's breakfast.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:195:"You've always been early. You load it on the three." She showed him, sitting, with a small turn of her one free shoulder. "I saw it the first week in that lane. I never said, for it worked. You'd have it ready, and then you'd go, and you never once went late. I thought it was how you did it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:215:It was the fifth one that showed him, as he stood breathing, with a goose regarding him from the trough.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:221:She had seen it in the same breath. She was sitting forward on the coping with her eyes narrowed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:229:He stood there for a breath with his mouth open. It had cost half of what it had ever cost him; and for once he had no need to reckon that afterward from an ache, as he reckoned every other price that he paid, since he had heard it while it was being spent, as plainly as a person hears the difference between a door slammed and a door shut.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-07-void-roads/manuscript/chapter-39.md:249:*Deployment since the Line: thirteen.*
