@@ -64,0 +65,38 @@
+On the night of the hundred and third day, when the others had gone up the ladder, Karis stayed on her keg in the middle of the barn with the hold's lantern and a horn cup.
+
+She had sent them away on purpose. She was fond of all four, and she had needed their hands; but there is a part of any counting that must be done by the one person who will have to swear to it.
+
+The barn ticked as the cold came into it, and a hen shifted in its sleep on the lid of the left-hand box. She tipped the cup out on the clay and counted the grains back into it, one at a time, by the lantern.
+
+It gave her a number. She wrote it small, in the corner of a leaf, and put her thumb over it. Then she took her thumb away and looked at it, since a researcher who cannot look at her own figure has no business holding a pencil.
+
+It was more than she had expected, and that was the first thing. She had sat at lectures on old hands. She owned copies of nine alphabets, in the left-hand box, three of them out of a restricted room; and the largest had fewer signs than she had grains. If these were letters, it was a prodigal sort of language. If they were not letters, then she did not know what the grouped marks were.
+
+She did not know, and she made herself say so over, there on the keg.
+
+The second thing was the bowl beside the cup.
+
+She had kept it for the doubtful ones, and there were a good many grains in it. A hook with two bars across the stem, and on another stone a hook with two bars set a hair closer. A long stroke crossed at a slant, and a long stroke crossed at a slant that leaned a little the other way. She had dropped each of those into the bowl, and gone on.
+
+Were they one kind, or two?
+
+She knew how she would have answered a week ago. She would have looked hard, and trusted her eye, and said *one*; and she would have been as sure of it as she had been of a stalk.
+
+She could still taste that apple. A small bald man had been talking about fruit, and it was the best sentence on method that anybody had said to her since Ternhall.
+
+She had one basket.
+
+Eleven stones, on one floor, rubbed in one afternoon by one hand. Every difference on those strips was a difference that she could not weigh. A wide hook might be a word. It might be a flaw in the grey, a variation in the cut, or her own pencil slipping while Lira shifted her palm. She could not test the cut. She could not go back to the stone.
