5:"I was fourteen. It was a district office on a cold morning, with a chalk circle on the boards for me to stand in. Something woke behind my breastbone, as it wakes in everybody on that day: a small light at the edge of my eye. You'd call it an Arbiter." He kept his voice low and his breath slow, as she had taught it to go. "It looked at me for eleven seconds, and I counted them. There's a plate let into the desk in those offices, which lights with the name of your Path. For eleven seconds it stayed dark."
33:"And this morning."
35:"This morning there's a tenth." He kept his eyes on her face, which hung over him upside down and grey. "It wants a real thing done to me for a long time, at a cost, by somebody who means it, and you had told me at Oxhollow what the long reading was: both hands, and all of you, for minutes. I knew when I lay down what it was likely to be. At the end there was a place in me standing open, and the thing that you read with was at the door. I could have kept that door shut. I'm better at shutting them than at anything else I do. I opened it."
57:"There's a leaf," said Cael. "I wrote it last night in the loft, before I had so much as crossed your yard. Everything that I knew is on it, and what I had made up my mind to do. You ought to read it before you answer, since it's worse than what I've told you and it's truer."
77:"That's the hundredth."
