147:"I know what you meant. You meant to be square. You'd take whatever I gave you and call the account shut." She shook her head, once. "You don't set it. That's the first of what it costs. You're not the one who knows the price, and you'll go about not knowing it until I do, which won't be today."
151:"And here's the second. You wrote that the real reason was that you didn't know what I'd say. I know what I'd have said. I knew it by the fourth line of that leaf." Her eyes were level and tired, and there was no heat in them at all. "It was mine to say, last night, through a door, and you took the saying. So I'm keeping the answer. You'll not have it today, and you'll not have it when you're old. You can carry that with the other ten."
173:"You've been a thing that nobody could read since you were fourteen. I've stood at that door five times and once more for twenty minutes, and I've a list to keep, and I came all the same." For the first time that morning he heard something in her voice that was not arithmetic. "I want to know what you find, and I want to be the one you tell."
191:It was still the hundredth morning, and the sun had not yet reached the well, when Oryn looked down and saw that she was holding a book.
201:Nobody had ever before told her the truth a night too late, in writing.
209:She had not said that part aloud. She had taken it for tiredness. In a long reading the healer goes along the patient and comes home to her own left hand, over and over, and toward the end she begins to hear herself as a person hears her own boots on a road at night. This had been like hearing a second pair. They had fallen exactly with hers, with the same short step on the right, where her heel was raw.
219:So perhaps it had taken nothing that could be weighed. He had told her that it never showed. She believed him; he had been at some pains, that morning, to tell her things that did him no good.
223:She gave herself away every day. That was all that her trade was. She found where a stranger's current was standing behind a break, and opened the way with her own, and held it open while six weeks of knitting went through in an hour; and she paid for the six weeks. An ox-driver had a foot today because she had been a little older by evening. She had never grudged one of them. It did not come from nowhere, and she had always known where it came from, and had chosen each time to spend it.
229:She had known it by the fourth line of his leaf, as she had told him, and she did not say it over now, even to herself, since a thing said over in an empty room is half given away. She turned the keeping of it over once instead, to be certain that she had her reasons straight and was not merely being hard. If the answer had been yes, then to tell him so was a pardon. If it had been no, it was a sentence, and a person can serve a sentence and be done. Either way he would have had from her, a day late, the very thing that he had gone round her to avoid, which was an end to not knowing. He had made her answer into a thing that never happened. It could stay so, and he could do his own carrying.
