75:Wind was the first, since Lira was asleep six feet from him and it had always leaned her way. It was a dry hiss, like the pale grass of the Fallow going over in a breath of air, and it lived in his feet. The read came after, low and even, under his soles: a millstone turning, heard through a floor. Something ticked in his right hand, as a lamp-wick ticks when the oil is poor, and he knew whose fire that was; and under his breastbone Pressure drew and filled, slowly, like a bellows worked by a tired arm that has a long day's iron still in front of it.
105:"The first night I thought I'd go mad. A forge doesn't go quiet when you bank it. The iron ticks while it cools, every piece at its own rate, and the coals settle, and the hide of the bellows creaks as it dries. There's water dripping off the tongs into the tub. I lay on a heap of sacks and I could have told you which sound was which with my eyes shut. I had the lot by heart inside an hour, and I never slept a wink."
107:"And the second night?"
109:"The same, near enough. The third I slept like a stone." He rubbed the four dark lines on his fingers with his thumb. "I asked the old man who swept for us how that was, for nothing in the shed had got any quieter. He said you never stop hearing a forge. You stop listening to it. And the day it makes a noise that you don't know, he said, you'll be on your feet with a bucket before you've woken up."
113:"So I'm to wait for the third night."
115:"I'd not know. I'm telling you about a shed." Brom got his legs up onto the boards. "But I'd leave off counting them, for a start. You know that there are nine, and you knew it yesterday. They'll still be nine when you wake."
121:The grain came on the hundred and first morning, in three carts from the river country, and by the middle of the forenoon it was going up the ladder past the place where they slept.
123:It was the last of the year's. The carters had come up through the stretch at noon the day before and slept in the cart-yard, three heavy men in sheepskins, with frost on their mules' eyelashes; and they wished to be unloaded and gone before the roads grew any harder. Holds farther up the valley would fetch that barley through the whole of the cold term, a cart at a time. Until then it lay in Thornwater's loft, under Thornwater's thatch, which was all that a grain hold was for.
125:It was also how the five paid for their bread there. Nobody had struck a bargain. The steading's woman had looked at Brom's shoulders on the first evening, and at the height of her loft, and had said that her own men were two short; and Brom had said that he would be glad of something to lift.
133:He had his left hand on the stone rim and the dipper in his right. He heard it begin, and that was new; it had shifted in him before, on a bluff and on a road and in a loft two nights ago, and each time he had known of it only as a ship's passenger knows that the cargo has slid. Now he heard the whole of it. The low sound rose, and went over from one side of him to the other in a long slow roll, as a sea goes over a bar, and it took as long about it as a person takes to draw a bucket. Then it lay down again, and where it lay was a little lower than before, and a little to one side.
137:He had been afraid of that weight since the day it came to him, and had lain awake more than once over what it might do if it ever rolled when he was not ready. He stood at a well with a dipper in his hand and heard it settle, and all that he felt was a kind of respect.
141:It was there. His boot came down on the frozen earth of the yard as it had come down all morning. But he felt it come down half a beat after it had done so, as a man hears an axe from across a valley after he has watched it fall.
173:He had not asked that thing for anything since a rut on a hill, and not once in a hundred days for practice. But this was a yard, at noon, inside a fence, where a healer mended with her door open and a researcher might light a lamp; and if he was to be half a beat behind himself on some road next week, he would sooner learn what that was like here, among geese.
241:He sat down beside her on the coping. His calves ached pleasantly, and the thing under his soles was still half a beat behind, and it no longer troubled him. He had spent a hundred days holding nine things shut; and until this noon he had never been able to hear what any one of them cost while he was spending it. A bill had always come afterward, in a forearm or a rib. This was the first time that he had been shown the price beforehand.
243:On the platform by the western gate the bowman had turned round. It was the one hour of the day when nothing had ever come up that valley, and he seemed to keep it for looking inward, at the hold. He had watched a boy fall about a yard eleven times, and get it right, and he stood with his bow at the length of his arm and gave no sign that it was any business of his. Then his eyes went to the girl on the coping, who had seen a fault from thirty feet with one arm tied, and they stayed there a good deal longer.
247:*Hundred and first day. The yard, at noon, inside the fence. Wind, eleven leans of a foot each, to learn it with the ground late. That is practice, and I am not putting it in the number; the number is what I spend on a road, or in a fight, or on a person. I enter it here so that nobody has to take my word for what's left out.*
255:She had been at it since the middle of the day, with the door of the back room standing open, and he had counted four go in and come out: a carter with a wrenched back, and a woman of the hold, and two that he did not know, who had walked in with the grain. She stopped her stride and a half from the coping, wiping her hands on her breeches. Lira had gone up to the loft by then, on her right hand and both knees.
257:"I did five yesterday," said Oryn. "Two that I mended and three that I told. Nothing came back short. They're what they were."
269:He told her. He kept it to what it was like, since he had nothing else: his own current going along at the edge of whatever he thought, in fits; and the nine coming up out of it singly, each with its own sound, so that he could have told her with his eyes shut which was which. He gave her two or three of the sounds, the grass and the millstone and the wick. He said that it had kept him awake, and that it did not pull at him as every one of the others had, and that the heaviest had moved over at noon to make room and had left the ground half a beat late.
