117:It was the coldest morning that they had yet had at that gate. The trough had frozen through to the stone in the night, so that Brom had broken it with the back of his hedging bill, and every breath in the yard went straight up. Lira was on the coping, where she had sat at that hour for ten mornings, with her foot set flat and her right hand shut on her knee.
121:He had done the sum on the ladder, and he did it again now, since he could not help it. If the eighth mark had turned, there would be a mending to pay for it, and then more days of watching on top of that. Every one of them would come off a round that was already sixteen behind, and be laid against a girl who had been told that she was not to carry them and would carry them regardless. If it had not turned, a healer went north in the morning.
127:Lira let out a breath that she seemed to have been holding for four days.
129:"All eight. That last one's caught up with the others. It was slow, and it isn't slow now." Oryn drew the wool up and tied it. "That changes nothing that I said at the stone. You've a fortnight behind you, and the rest to do. Nothing in that hand till I say."
139:"That bone's a month old today. I said a month, on the mountain. It may run."
151:"You I can tell nothing. You'd know before I would." She rubbed her palms together. "That's sixteen out of this gate. A round never gives a day back; it takes one."
153:He had that sum by heart, from the ninety-fifth day to the morning after a fortnight. Sixteen and one made seventeen, somewhere on a road that he had never seen; and she did not say where the one would fall.
157:She settled the matter of the mule before the middle of the morning, and she settled it as she settled the order of a bench.
159:Cael had not seen that it was a matter. He had known for eleven days that three boxes stood in that loft at a hundredweight each, that the carter who had brought them east was gone home with grain, and that the ferry was two days off. He had put all of it where he put the things that would be dealt with when there was leisure, behind a table and a birthday and a week of slips.
169:"He'd carry one. He'd say it was nothing, and he'd be grey by noon."
181:"I've had her thirty rounds, and she's a mule. She'd as soon carry boxes as panniers. Boxes don't want mending at the end of the day." The healer spoke more quickly than she was used to, and looked at none of them. "There's a beast at every gate on my round. They feed mine, and one of them shoes her. The next has a jenny that's eaten her head off since the barley was in and done nothing for it. I'll have that one off them, and they'll thank me. You've nobody who'd lend you so much as a goat."
187:He shut his mouth, since it was the second time in ten days that he had been told that he was not the one who set a price.
205:"I rode her down a mountain for four days," she said, "and I wouldn't say that she'd an eye. I said that she was a saddle."
213:The stake was moved to sixty paces at noon, and half of Thornwater found that it had business near the western gate.
227:"I know that it's left. It was left on the hundred and fourth."
265:He had had the order of it for fifteen days. He had settled it on the evening that five people stopped at a rise and he did not: there was a thing that he would have to say, when he had it, and Lira was to hear it first, and after her the rest. He had not kept to that. He had told a healer the oldest part of it on a table, since that debt would not wait. But the part that was Lira's he had carried past a stone, and a rut, and ten nights in a loft; and she had let him carry it.
269:"He said to keep it till he'd eaten," she said. She kept her eyes on the valley. "He said that nothing's come up to that stone since our ford. Fifty-seven evenings. He's stood here for every one."
--- ch44 day mentions ---
3:The healer came up the loft ladder that night for the first time, and looked at a wall of barley with a row of blankets under it.
11:Cael gave it to them standing. He gave it as he had given it to one person on a gate-walk an hour before, in the same order and in fewer words: the noon of the ninety-sixth, and a read that had run across a stride, and a foot of Wind at a boulder; a row for a table that had been two true halves; and that he did not know why any of it was so.
13:"Lira's had that since dusk. The rest of you have it now. It's fourteen days late, and I'm sorry for every one of them." He looked at the square woman by the ladder. "And there's one thing more that I have, from the night of the birthday, which none of you is getting. I read myself, as she set it. What I found is hers before it's anybody's. I'm telling you that it exists, so that nobody learns later that I sat on it."
21:"Somebody had to give one. I was the only person there who knew that it was a different ruling for me." He made himself go on. "I spent a quarter of an hour that I'd said I wouldn't, and I let six people stand at a den believing that all six were alike. If I had told you at the stone, at noon, you'd have planned for it. We'd have been in that lane by two hands, and Lira would have two arms."
27:"I have a row to amend," she said. Her voice was whole. "*Stated by him, not tested by me* stands, since it is still true. I am adding beneath it: *tested by him, at noon on the 96th, unwitnessed; told on the 110th*. And now I want something that I have never had. I have had that hillside for fourteen nights in five heads, and not one account of it on paper. I want what each of you saw. In the order in which you saw it. Nothing that you supposed."
53:Her eyes came to Cael then, and rested on him for the length of a breath. He had watched her cross a grey stone eight times at dawn, alone, and had shut a book on it; and he saw that she knew he had kept it shut.
73:Cael waited for one of them to join those things together. He had sat in a good many rooms where it would have been done before the lamp was trimmed: a seat would have found a word for it, and a clerk would have written the word down, and by morning there would have been a file. Here six people sat round one lantern with a made floor, and an age that had no ruler, and a boy; and each of them left the three lying apart, as Karis had left them on a trestle, since nobody in that loft knew what lay between them and nobody was willing to pretend.
79:She put her finger on the bend, two days east of a square with a dot.
81:"That one I have walked. I have its round, and its radius, and eleven sheets." The finger moved a long way west and south, to a blank among the carter's hill-hatching. "That is a day south of the ferry and a little aside; I rang it on the ninety-second evening, and wrote *guess*." It moved again, far down toward the corner of the hide, where the strokes of his water lay thick. "That is a long way south and east, with no road inside a day of it, and it is a guess as well. And the fourth is not on this skin at all." She lifted her eyes to the loft door. "It is on a sheet that he counted once, as a boy, and cannot place. One sheet had two."
107:"North in the morning," Oryn said. It was not an answer to Karis; it was a fact about a route. "Oxhollow's seventeen days late and the old woman's knee doesn't care what's under a floor. I'll be on the road at first light and I'll be three days north by the time you're back at the board." She looked at him. "Thornwater's on my route. You know where that is now."
117:Brom had the ladder down. She went to the head of it, where Seln had stood all evening with his back to the yard, and she put her hand on his shoulder.
123:Brom had not drawn it up again. He sat at the head of it with his legs hanging into the barn, as he had sat on the first night of the listening, and when Cael came to the edge he went on looking down into the dark where the mule was.
125:"Ten," he said. "I was the third of them, and I've been by you for every one since, or near enough. I've been afraid of a good many things in that time. Dogs. A string. A letter." He rubbed the four dark lines with his thumb. "I went over it tonight, while she was at her wheres, to be sure of my figures. I was never once afraid of you."
137:Cael stood at the loft door for some time afterward, with the frost coming in round his feet and the yard white below him. A boat had gone west on the hundred and second day with a clerk's fair copy in it, and that was eight days ago. He supposed that by now it lay on somebody's desk.
141:Vastin received the broker's completion report as a courtesy copy, forwarded through a registry office that had no interest in any board east of the Line, in the second folder of the morning, marked *Fallow Ring: crew returned, instruments not recovered*. He read it twice — a broker's report from a town on no registry map, on his desk with no routing note — and did not know why it had reached him, and filed that, in the register of a man who had never once in forty years pretended to knowledge he lacked, as the whole of what he knew about it. He returned the copy to its folder unannotated.
157:*The reading turned outward. I ask first. Every time. If I ever don't, that's the day I became the instrument.*
167:He had the next thing ready, and he had had it ready for eight days.
169:"You said that you'd say when, and you go in an hour. I read myself on the night of the hundred and second, as you set it, alone and on my back. I found—"
181:"Once is a morning."
185:"I'd a teacher who struck my knuckles for less than that. You put your hands on a thing one time, and you've learned what sort of morning you were having; you've not learned the thing." Her eyes were level in the grey. "Were you frightened?"
189:"And you've not gone back to it in eight days. I'm not asking you why, since I can see why from here. So you'll go back. You'll read it three times, on three different days, with a night between each. Read it frightened, and read it when you're not, and then you'll know what's there and what was only you." She buckled the strap. "After that you tell me, all of it at once and in order. You don't give me a piece of it now because I'm leaving and you'd like to have paid."
211:*Hers. Three readings of myself, on three different days. Then all of it to her, at once. Nobody on the road, to find out whether I can.*
217:He stayed at the well until the grey had come into the east. Then he turned back sixty leaves, to a line that he had set down under an inn's eave on the forty-ninth night, when he had counted himself free. He read that through, and came forward again to the clean leaf, and wrote the one that had to stand across from it.
225:Oryn went out by the northern wicket as the hundred and eleventh day was breaking, and for the first quarter of a mile she kept putting her hand out for a cheek-strap.
227:It was not there, and she had known that it would not be. She had walked that track thirty times with a small brown head at her right shoulder, and her hand had thirty rounds of habit in it; it went out at every turn of the path, and closed on air, and came back. She let it. A person who has set a great many bones does not expect a habit to knit in a morning.
229:It was a hard grey dawn, with the frost standing an inch deep on the grazing, and her own breath went up in front of her at the pace that she walked. Behind her a hold was waking. She heard the sweep of the well creak, and a goose, and the bar being drawn at the western gate for five people and three boxes.
235:"Sixteen days too long."
243:She would need it tomorrow night. There was a jenny at the next gate that was the worst-led beast on seven roads.
245:She did her sum as she climbed. She did it every morning, as another person might say her name over.
247:Out of that gate on the hundred and eleventh, when she had said the ninety-fifth: sixteen. Two days of road to the next. Two gates after it, and an old woman on the way whose knee had never once kept to a healer's days; that was where the odd one would go. Then the ford with the oxen, where they had last seen her on the eighty-fourth and had looked for her on the hundred and fourth. She would come down to it on the hundred and twenty-first, if nothing broke, and that was seventeen.

